course: dictionary
lesson: 4940
title: Impossible: three meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word impossible has three meanings, and the words around it tell you which one you need. These nearby words are the context, and they leave clues.
explain: «It's impossible to be in two places at once.» Here impossible means not able to happen. «You're impossible today!» is different: it means very difficult to deal with, usually about behaviour, not about what can be done.
explain: To check, swap in another meaning and see if the sentence still makes sense. In «They tried to do the impossible», the impossible is a noun meaning something that cannot be done.
fact: Impossible means not able to happen or be done.
fact: In maths, an impossible event has probability 0, like rolling a 7 on a normal dice.
fact: The probability scale runs from impossible (0), through unlikely, even chance and likely, to certain (1).
fact: The prefix im- is used before many words starting with m or p, such as impossible, immature and impatient.
fact: The noun «the impossible» means something that cannot be done, as in «They tried to do the impossible».
exercise: «It's impossible to be in two places at once.» What does impossible mean here? | Very cheap | *Not able to happen | Very difficult to deal with | Hard to see in the dark
exercise: «You're impossible today!» What does impossible suggest? | Cannot be eaten | Hard to see in the dark | *Very difficult to deal with | Very wet
exercise: «They tried to do the impossible.» What is the impossible here? | *Something that cannot be done | The name of a team | Something very easy to finish | A type of sport
test: In maths, what probability does an impossible event have? | *0 | 1 | 100 | Half
test: Which word is a clear example of im- used before m or p? | unhappy | insect | *immature | incorrect
exam: «It is impossible to grow food with no insects at all.» What does impossible mean here? | Very difficult to deal with | Very cheap | *Not able to happen | Easy to finish
